{
  "$comment": [
    "NBA 2K27 CAP BREAKERS.",
    "",
    "READ THIS BEFORE ADDING DATA — the column headings in the app are misleading, and misreading them",
    "put wrong 'verified' caps in this dataset once already (see meta.json 0.7.0).",
    "",
    "The Cap Breaker page shows, per attribute: an 'Original Cap', five slots, and a 'New Cap'.",
    "",
    "  - 'Original Cap' is NOT the body's ceiling. It is what THIS PLAYER ALLOCATED to that attribute.",
    "    Three transcribed builds prove it: Ball Handle reads 70 on a 6'5\" guard, 86 on a 6'8\" guard and",
    "    75 on a 6'11\" big. No body-cap model makes a 6'5\" guard the worst ball handler of the three. It",
    "    tracks the ARCHETYPE instead — the 6'8\" is a Playmaking Creator, the 6'5\" an Off-Screen Shooter",
    "    who never dribbles. Same story for Pass Accuracy (65 / 87 / 77) and Speed With Ball (73 / 80 / 68).",
    "",
    "  - Each of the five slots is worth a DIFFERENT, PER-ATTRIBUTE amount, and gains diminish down the",
    "    row. Post Control on the 6'5\" build runs +13 / +11 / +9 / +7 / +6.",
    "",
    "  - A LOCKED slot (null here, a padlock in the app) means the ladder has run into the body's real",
    "    ceiling. So a row that ends in a lock tells you the CAP EXACTLY — its New Cap is the cap. A row",
    "    that spends all five slots without locking merely ran out of slots, and its New Cap is only a",
    "    lower bound. This is the only way the app exposes true caps, and caps.json is derived from it.",
    "",
    "  - 'MAX' appears instead of a number when the ladder reaches 99. Recorded here as newCap 99.",
    "",
    "BECAUSE THE LADDER IS RELATIVE TO THE ALLOCATION, a gain table belongs to a BUILD, not to a body.",
    "Two players on the same frame with different allocations see different ladders. Entries are therefore",
    "keyed by build, carry `body` and `sampledAt`, and the planner applies a row only when the build's",
    "current rating matches `sampledAt`. Anything else would be extrapolation.",
    "",
    "HOW TO ADD A BUILD: open it in the NBA 2K HQ app, go to Cap Breakers, and transcribe each row as",
    "`\"<attribute_id>\": { \"slots\": [s1..s5], \"newCap\": N }`, null for a padlock, plus the Original Cap",
    "column into `sampledAt`. The loader checks sampledAt + slots === newCap, rejects an unlocked slot",
    "after a locked one, and cross-checks every locked row against the derived cap in caps.json."
  ],
  "verification": {
    "status": "verified",
    "source": "NBA 2K HQ app MyPLAYER builder Cap Breaker page (https://apps.apple.com/app/id6745190566), screenshots supplied 2026-08-25.",
    "notes": "The five-slot structure, the per-attribute variable gains, the diminishing pattern, the locked slots and the fact that a lock marks the body's true ceiling are all read directly off the app across three builds. What is NOT known is how many breakers a player may actually apply — see `allocation`."
  },
  "rules": {
    "visibleOnlyAt99Overall": true,
    "grantsBadgeTokens": false,
    "verification": {
      "status": "community-verified",
      "source": "2K27 builder coverage: cap breaker amounts are not viewable until 99 overall, and applying them does not provide additional badge tokens."
    },
    "note": "grantsBadgeTokens=false is why the optimizer treats cap breakers as a pure post-build overlay: a breaker can push an attribute over a badge threshold but cannot pay for the badge."
  },
  "enabled": true,
  "slotsPerAttribute": 5,
  "costsBuildPoints": false,
  "absoluteCeiling": 99,
  "allocation": {
    "$comment": [
      "THE ONE THING THE SCREENSHOTS DO NOT SETTLE. The app shows five slots on every attribute row and a",
      "New Cap that assumes all five are filled. Two readings fit that display equally well:",
      "",
      "  'shared-pool'   — you earn a limited number of breakers and choose where they go.",
      "  'per-attribute' — every attribute's five slots fill independently, so New Cap is reachable everywhere.",
      "",
      "'per-attribute' would hand the 6'5\" build over +250 attribute points for free, which is a very large",
      "claim to make on the strength of a display that is equally consistent with scarcity. The default is",
      "'shared-pool': it can under-promise, never over-promise. Switch `mode` if the generous reading is right.",
      "",
      "`poolSize` is a guess and is the single least-supported number in this file."
    ],
    "mode": "shared-pool",
    "poolSize": 5,
    "verification": {
      "status": "unverified",
      "notes": "Neither the mode nor the pool size is sourced. The GAIN TABLE is verified; how many of those gains you may claim is not."
    }
  },
  "eligibility": {
    "mode": "all-except",
    "excludedAttributes": [],
    "note": "mode may be 'all', 'all-except' (uses excludedAttributes) or 'only' (uses includedAttributes). Per-attribute lock-out is expressed by null slots in the gain table, not here."
  },
  "includedAttributes": [],
  "gainTables": {
    "$comment": [
      "Keyed by BUILD, not by body — the ladder depends on what the player allocated (see the file header).",
      "Each entry carries `body` (`<POSITION>|<height>|<weight>|<wingspan>`) and `sampledAt` (the allocation).",
      "",
      "The planner matches on `body` first, then applies only those rows whose `sampledAt` equals the build's",
      "current rating. A body with no entry, or an allocation nothing was sampled at, gets NO plan rather than",
      "a guess: gains run from +1 to +13 across attributes on a single build."
    ],
